# Custom Page Document Compatibility

## Authority

- `pages.content` is the sole custom-page definition and interactive-record
  authority. It is a JSON array saved through `PUT /pages/{id}`.
- There is no normalized section read/write path, page palette, shadow
  projection, typed section CRUD API, or generated cross-language schema.
- Ordinary saves lock the page row and merge current interactive `records` into
  the incoming document before updating it. Interactive mutations lock and
  rewrite the same document.
- `PUT /pages/{id}` preserves omitted fields. Directory metadata edits omit
  `content`; builder saves continue sending the complete authoritative document.
- `/pages/browse` exposes bounded content-free summaries. The intent-only
  `/pages/browse/{id}/preview` read returns a service-authorized and
  caller-sanitized copy without creating a second content authority.
- Proposal, showcase, and event publications reference owned `pages` rows rather
  than storing a second document. Community detail uses the same validation and
  caller-specific interactive sanitization boundary as the canonical page route.
  Embedded community documents receive their linked page ID, so authored form,
  Q&A, survey, poll, and vote sections submit through the ordinary page-response
  API without granting embedded design or manager controls.
- The same publication transaction creates its separate author-owned forum
  thread. Discussion stays authoritative in the forum domain and is linked from
  community detail; it is never copied into the page document.

## Stored envelope

Each section retains the fields its renderer needs. The common shape is:

```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "display_order": 1,
  "section_type": "hero",
  "heading": "",
  "subheading": "",
  "config": "{}",
  "items": []
}
```

Compatibility is intentional:

- section and item IDs may be positive JSON integers or non-empty strings;
- config may be an encoded JSON object string or a JSON object;
- unknown section, item, and config fields survive reads and unrelated edits;
- `features` remains a stored alias for the `feature_grid` renderer;
- identity is page-scoped and preserves JSON type (`1` and `"1"` differ);
- new browser-created records currently use numeric IDs and encoded configs.

## Registry

Backend `internal/s_registry` and frontend `sectionRegistry.ts` list the 20
renderer types and presentation metadata. They are allowlists and dispatch
registries, not a claim that every config shares one schema.

`resource_embed` stores only a supported resource type, the selected
product/thread identifier or documentation slug. Its renderer
reads the existing store, forum, and visibility-aware documentation APIs; page
JSON never becomes a copied resource authority. Picker lists retain the source
API bounds, exact
resources subscribe through their existing realtime keys, and reconnect or
invalidation refetches remain authoritative.
Product embeds and product pages share one media-carousel/lightbox owner, so
every product media item retains the same cycling and preview behavior.
Documentation embeds resolve the first visible guide and render it through the
shared documentation shell's bounded embedded variant; navigation and content
scroll within the embed instead of expanding into full-page chrome.
Browse previews render an inert reference-shaped fallback and never fan out
source-domain enrichment reads or subscriptions.

The frontend registry maps each canonical type to a lazy renderer and clipboard
sanitizer. Unknown types receive a visible safe fallback and remain removable
by editors without exposing config.

## Shared UI

`SectionFrame` owns layout, spacing, animation, literal/inherited colors,
collapsing, toolbar composition, viewport animation, and child mount deferral.
The editor's insertion control opens one responsive modal section picker. Its
static frontend metadata covers every registered type exactly once, gives every
category and type a description, and supports description-aware search plus
list/grid presentation without changing the stored section contract.
Section-type labels identify blocks in the editor only. New sections start with
an empty optional heading, and viewers suppress the exact legacy picker label
previously persisted as a heading; deliberately authored headings remain part of
the rendered content.
Animation observation remains reversible and separate from one-shot renderer
activation. The first section is eager; later sections use renderer-shaped
skeletons and a near-viewport boundary, while preview sections use the clipped
thumbnail as their observer root. The hero fallback preserves the real
500px/400px full-bleed silhouette; collection fallbacks retain their actual
text-only, icon-led, or media-led hierarchy instead of sharing one generic card.
Each eligible `MediaPlaceholder` directly opens the shared lightbox. URL-backed
image, video, and audio frames are pointer/keyboard activatable even when loading
fails; decorative, nested-lightbox, and inert preview callers explicitly opt out.
Its config helper:

- accepts both config encodings;
- reads established camelCase and snake_case aliases;
- patches only shared appearance keys;
- retains unknown keys;
- writes an encoded config string after an appearance edit.

Reusable datasource-backed section presets remain in the existing
`custom_sections` feature and `/config/section-presets` compatibility alias.
Their component-group configuration remains inside the preset JSON: the
datasource editor can reopen and update that record without copying it into the
page document, while custom-section blocks link back to the owning preset editor.
Component order, percentage width, literal text bindings, and stat icon position
are presentation fields within that existing configuration.

Domain-owned workflows may project an `InteractiveConfig` through the shared
`InteractiveActionSection` binding. The binding reuses the interactive field,
validation, idempotency-intent, loading, and feedback UI but delegates mutation
to the domain endpoint. It must not append domain responses to page JSON or turn
`pages.content` into a second workflow, attendance, commerce, or moderation
authority.

## Validation and security

- Saves require a JSON array, unique valid IDs, supported section types, valid
  integration references, component-registry constraints, and bounded
  interactive definitions.
- Interactive responses are never included in public page payloads beyond the
  caller-specific sanitized projection.
- Duplicate pages clear interactive records.
- Page WebSocket events are content-free invalidations.
- HTML and component output retain their existing sanitization boundaries.
- Resource embeds never bypass source-domain visibility or mutation policy and
  expose only canonical read links; they do not add iframe or remote-URL embeds.

## Retirement guard

Migration `035_retire_normalized_page_sections.sql` refuses to drop the unused
experimental tables if normalized response rows or palette tokens exist. Empty
shadow projections, quarantine, themes, and cutover telemetry are disposable.

